      "question_id": 6,
      "question": "Which red flags in acute low back pain mandate imaging or referral?",
      "status": "ANSWERED",
      "answer": "In acute low back pain, the red flags that mandate imaging or referral are:\n\n* Saddle anaesthesia, bladder/bowel involvement, or progressive weakness [STW_ORT_01_Acute_Low_Back_Pain.pdf, p1].\n* Fever with spinal tenderness [STW_ORT_01_Acute_Low_Back_Pain.pdf, p1].\n* Significant trauma [STW_ORT_01_Acute_Low_Back_Pain.pdf, p1].\n* Age >65 with new pain and weight loss [STW_ORT_01_Acute_Low_Back_Pain.pdf, p1].\n\n*(Note: Pain that is persistent for >6 weeks also indicates an imaging pathway [STW_ORT_01_Acute_Low_Back_Pain.pdf, p1]).*",
